WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
"Is Black Pepper Good for You?"

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.040
Black pepper,
the so-called "King of Spices,"

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.320
is the most commonly used spice
in the world,

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.800
thanks to piperine,
the compound responsible

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.720
for black pepper's distinct
biting quality,

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.320
as well as some of pepper's
propitious properties,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.440
such as acting as a bioenhancer,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.560
improving the absorption
of other compounds.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.880
Dr. Greger has talked about

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.960
how just a tiny pinch
of black pepper--

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.840
1/20th of a single teaspoon--

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.680
can boost the bioavailability
into the bloodstream

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.680
of curcumin, the yellow pigment
of the spice turmeric,

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.880
such that
adding turmeric to the diet

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.160
with a pinch of pepper

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.160
may be more effective

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.840
in reducing oxidative stress
and inflammation

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.040
than just turmeric alone.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.320
Crack about a quarter teaspoon
of black pepper

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.440
onto your vegetables,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.240
and significantly more
carotenoid phytonutrients

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.960
will make it
into your bloodstream

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.720
than if you ate
the same vegetables without it.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.080
But one of the ways piperine
enhances bioavailability

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.120
is by inhibiting
the detox enzymes

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.040
in our liver and intestines,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.040
thereby suppressing
our body's ability

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.000
to get rid
of foreign substances,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.680
even good ones
like curcumin and beta-carotene.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.680
Could that be a bad thing
for people taking certain drugs?

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.680
A systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.040
looked at the effects of typical
daily doses of black pepper

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.040
on conventional drugs,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.680
and indeed
it increases the bioavailability

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.920
of a variety of common ones,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.560
including Dilantin, Inderal,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.760
and an asthma drug
called theophylline.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.680
Now, on the one hand,

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.200
that may allow us to give
smaller doses of the drugs,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.280
but on the other hand,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.320
inhibiting
our natural drug detox enzymes

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:46.880
might cause serious consequences

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.800
if it led to drug levels
that are too high.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.880
Grapefruit and grapefruit juice
have similar effects,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.680
described as
a "pharmacologist's nightmare,"

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.640
which may help explain why those
who increase consumption

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.760
by just a half a serving a day

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.840
may go on to live shorter lives.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.240
I couldn't find any such study

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.360
tracking black pepper
consumption and mortality,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.040
but if you are on medication

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.480
and use more than
a pinch of pepper a day,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.411
please tell your prescribing
health professional.

